From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 11:50:48 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] DsRunner: write /_pico/igr-log.txt and show when the hotkey
 is recognized

A hotkey that does nothing on real hardware gives no hint why: the patch
may have been left out (switched off, no dispatcher found, no patch
space), the reset may wait for the slot 1 lock, or the loader may not
even be this build.

The arm9 now records what the in-game reset search and patch did
(sdk version, result per dispatcher version, hook address, OS_ResetSystem,
why it was not applied, patch space needed) in a word-only struct, since
the arm9 runs from vram. The arm7 asks for it with a new IPC command
after the arm9 patches are done, before the game starts, and writes
/_pico/igr-log.txt with the build id, boot mode, rom, game code and
title, platform, DLDI cache state, launcher path and where it came from,
and whether /_pico/igr.off was seen. A normal start rewrites the file;
a start after a reset appends to it, so the record of the game survives
the return to the launcher. Homebrew starts are logged before the
"DLDI driver not found" error, which is where a return without a cached
driver ends.

When the keys have been held long enough, the key check sets the main
screen's master brightness half way to white before entering the reset.
A bright screen that keeps running means the reset waits for the slot 1
lock. The reset part itself is unchanged, so it still fits the small
patch space gaps.

loader/build.sh passes DSRUNNER_BUILD_ID to make; without it the log
says "unknown".

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_018ba2i77rK9QaTUSsETPJXV
